# Copy to .env.local and fill in from the Supabase dashboard (Project Settings → API keys).
# .env.local is gitignored, and none of this belongs on OneDrive.

# The project URL and its publishable key. Both are browser-safe: the publishable key
# identifies the project, and every query it makes is still subject to RLS. Next inlines
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ variables into the client bundle, which is exactly what these are for.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project.supabase.co
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=sb_publishable_...

# Bypasses RLS completely. Per CLAUDE.md it is for provisioning the one account with
# `npm run account:provision`, and for the auth tests against a real project, on the server
# laptop only. Ingest does not use it: see ROLODECK_INGEST_DATABASE_URL below. It must never
# reach a browser, a client bundle, OneDrive, or GitHub. Deliberately not NEXT_PUBLIC_.
SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY=sb_secret_...

# Optional, and only for running the auth integration tests against a real project rather
# than the in-process stub. Point these at a scratch Supabase project, never at the one
# holding real Profiles: the tests create and delete users through the Admin API.
# SUPABASE_TEST_URL=https://your-scratch-project.supabase.co
# SUPABASE_TEST_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=sb_publishable_...

# Postgres connection string. The app's own reads and writes go through it, as the
# `authenticated` role — see docs/adr/0005 — so it is needed to run the app as well as
# `npm run db:migrate`. Use the pooled connection string from the Supabase dashboard
# (Project Settings → Database). It carries a password: deliberately not NEXT_PUBLIC_, and not
# on OneDrive. The tests need no database at all; they run one in-process.
DATABASE_URL=postgres://...

# The user id of the one account, from `npm run account:provision` or the Supabase dashboard
# (Authentication → Users). Ingest stamps it on every Profile it writes. Not a secret, but not
# optional either: a Profile written with the wrong owner is not wrong-looking, it is invisible
# — no RLS policy matches it — and ingest bypasses RLS, so nothing would report it.
ROLODECK_OWNER_ID=00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000

# The Postgres connection every ingest script (`npm run ingest:*`, `npm run source:show-hn`)
# writes through — `getIngestDb()` in db/ingest-connection.ts — logging in as the
# `rolodeck_ingest` role that migration 0008 creates. See docs/adr/0013.
#
# What it may do: select, insert and update `profiles`, `news_items`, `events` and
# `event_attendances`, bypassing RLS on those four tables only; delete Attendance; and ask
# `ingest.kept_profile_ids` which Company Profiles are Kept. Nothing else: it cannot read or
# write `swipes`, `user_profiles` or anything in `auth`, cannot delete or truncate a Company
# Profile, and cannot become any other role. It is refused unless its user is `rolodeck_ingest`
# (or `rolodeck_ingest.<project-ref>` through Supabase's pooler), so the `postgres` connection
# string cannot be pasted in here by mistake.
#
# It is the only database credential that belongs in GitHub Actions secrets. DATABASE_URL and
# SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY never go there. Deliberately not DATABASE_URL, and not NEXT_PUBLIC_.
ROLODECK_INGEST_DATABASE_URL=postgres://rolodeck_ingest.your-project-ref:password@your-pooler-host:5432/postgres

# An email address the SEC can reach you at, for `npm run ingest:sec-form-d`. EDGAR answers
# 403 to a client that sends no User-Agent, and blocks ones it cannot contact, so this is
# required rather than defaulted — and it is deliberately not compiled in, because it should
# be the address of whoever is actually making the requests. Not a secret; not NEXT_PUBLIC_
# either, since nothing in the browser fetches from the SEC.
SEC_EDGAR_CONTACT=you@example.com

# The GNews API key News fetches articles with, for `npm run ingest:news`, from
# https://gnews.io (Dashboard). Server laptop only: it spends a daily quota on the account that
# owns it, so it is deliberately not NEXT_PUBLIC_, it is sent in a header rather than a URL so
# nothing that logs a request logs it, and `npm run check:bundle-secrets` fails the build if it
# reaches a browser. The app itself never reads it — the News page reads stored articles.
GNEWS_API_KEY=your-gnews-api-key
